.TH TIMESYNC 8
.SH NAME
timesync \- set the clock from a GPS receiver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B timesync
[
.B -n
.I samples
] [
.B -o
.I ms
] [
.B -t
.I ms
] [
.B -p
.I seconds
] [
.I timefile
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Timesync
sets this host's clock from the time a GPS receiver gives, through the
.B time
file of
.IR gpsfs (4)
(default
.BR /mnt/gps/time ;
if that is not mounted here, the posted
.B #sgps/fs
is mounted in a name space of the command's own).
It is for a host with no network time, as
.IR sntp (8)
needs, and no clock of its own that keeps time: a board in the field.
.PP
The time file pairs the time of the last fix with this host's clock
when the fix's first sentence began to arrive.
A sentence arrives after the time it gives, never before, and by a
delay that varies; so of
.I samples
fixes (5, each one second's), the one whose offset is largest was the
least delayed, and its offset is taken, as Plan 9's
.I timesync
does.
Only fixes marked
.B sync=yes
count: never a recording's, a feed's, or one whose time this host
stamped (a receiver giving none, as GDL 90 and Garmin's Aviation
output do).
.PP
What is left is the receiver's own delay between the second and the
sentence giving it, tens to hundreds of milliseconds by receiver;
.B -o
adds a correction in milliseconds, measured once against a better
clock.
.PP
If the clock is off by more than
.B -t
milliseconds (200) it is stepped, through
.B /dev/time
.RI ( cons (3)).
.I Timesync
says by how much, and whether it set the clock or left it.
With
.B -p
it samples every
.I seconds
and steps when it must, saying so only then.
.SH EXAMPLES
On a Raspberry Pi with a receiver on its serial port and no network:
.IP
.EX
gpsfs source '#t/eia0' 9600
timesync -p 600 &
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/timesync.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR gpsfs (4),
.IR cons (3),
.IR sntp (8)
.SH BUGS
NMEA gives time to tens of milliseconds at best.
A receiver's pulse per second, which would give it to microseconds, is
not read.
.PP
The clock is stepped, not slewed.
